# Lapila stdlib: Stamped and the PoolManager, links to an entity that see when it has ended
#
# `node.stamp()` on an entity gives a `Stamped[Node]`, 8 bytes: a slot in the PoolManager's table and the slot's
# generation. The entity keeps its slot in a hidden U32 the builder adds to its type (only to the entity types the
# program stamps). The slot holds the entity's address while it lives. When the entity ends (`drop`, the builder's give
# back of a STACK place, the old value of `y <- v`, the walk of the entity that held it), the slot's generation goes up
# and its address is cleared, so every stamp of it fails. When the entity moves to another address (`steal` out of its
# place, `return` of a STACK place, a collection that moves its elements), the slot keeps its generation and gets the
# new address: the builder clears the address where the entity leaves its place and writes the new one where it lands
# (every write of a whole value to memory), so a stamp fails only while the entity is between places. `fetch()` gives
# the entity's `@T`, or throws `StaleStampError` when the generations differ or the entity is between places. A stamp
# doesn't keep the entity alive and copies for free. Two stamps compare with `===` / `!==` (the same entity), never `==`.
#
# The table is chunks of 4,096 entries of 16 bytes (64 KiB) that never move, and a directory of the chunks'
# addresses that doubles when it is full. The directory it replaces stays until the program ends. A fetch reads the
# directory, the chunk and the entry with no lock, and reads the entry once: a program on one thread can't end the
# entity between the reads. Taking and ending slots take a short lock. Slot 0 is never used, so an all-zero stamp is
# always stale. A slot whose generation reaches U32.MAX is retired, never used again. The PoolManager is a service:
# `PoolManager.set_up(memory: m)` in `start`, before the first `stamp()`.

module System/Memory

### The address of the directory: one Addr per chunk. Exported, with POOL_NEXT, for a language runtime that reads a
### stamp's entry in its own code (Revare's fetch, Ingrid/lapila/stamps.li): the same reads as `PoolManager.lookup`.
@export("lapila_stamp_directory")
secret global POOL_DIRECTORY: &Addr
### How many chunk addresses the directory has room for.
secret global POOL_DIRECTORY_ROOM: &U64
### How many chunks there are.
secret global POOL_CHUNKS: &U64
### The slot after the last one ever taken, 0 before the first.
@export("lapila_stamp_next")
secret global POOL_NEXT: &U32
### The first free slot, 0 for none.
secret global POOL_FREE: &U32
### 1 while a flow takes or ends a slot.
secret global POOL_LOCK: &U32
### Where the chunks come from, None before set_up.
secret global POOL_MEMORY: &(@MemoryManager?)

### How many entries a chunk holds.
secret preset POOL_CHUNK_SLOTS: U64 = 4096

### A link to an entity that doesn't keep it alive: the entity's slot in the PoolManager's table and the slot's
### generation when the stamp was taken. Made with `stamp()` on the entity, read with `fetch()`. Copied for free.
### A stamp always points at an entity that existed: there is no empty stamp, and `fetch()` fails only once the entity
### has ended. A link that may be missing is a `Stamped[T]?`, taken with `try link.fetch()`.
### :typeparam T: The entity's type.
record Stamped[T]
    ### The entity's slot in the table.
    secret slot: U32
    ### The slot's generation when the stamp was taken.
    secret generation: U32

### A stamp whose entity has ended (its slot has a newer generation now), or is between two places.
crashable StaleStampError
    nothing

### The table of the program's stamps, one per program. Its routines are called on the type, and its state is its own
### globals. A program that stamps sets it up first, in `start`: `PoolManager.set_up(memory: HEAP)`.
record PoolManager
    nothing

### One entry of the table: 16 bytes.
secret record PoolEntry
    ### The entity's address while it lives, zero after it ends.
    at: Addr
    ### Goes up each time the entity in the slot ends.
    generation: U32
    ### The next free slot after this one, while this one is free.
    next_free: U32

### Writes `The entity this stamp was taken of has ended, or is between two places.`
### :typeparam TWriter: The Writer's type.
### :param out: Where it goes.
routine StaleStampError.message_into[TWriter](out: @TWriter) -> None
needs TWriter obeys Writer
    out.write("The entity this stamp was taken of has ended, or is between two places.")
    return

### Sets the table up: its chunks come from `memory`. A program calls it once, in `start`, before its first `stamp()`.
### :param memory: The memory manager the chunks come from.
### :throws: Throws `UnacceptedValueError` when the table is set up already.
common routine PoolManager.set_up(memory: @MemoryManager) -> None
    lock_pool()
    when POOL_MEMORY.load()
        is @MemoryManager =>
            unlock_pool()
            throw UnacceptedValueError(description: "The PoolManager is set up already: call PoolManager.set_up(memory: m) once, in start.")
        is None => nothing
    POOL_MEMORY.store(memory)
    unlock_pool()
    return

### Whether `set_up` was called: a language runtime sets the table up on its first stamp.
### :returns: True once the table is set up.
common routine PoolManager.is_set_up() -> Bool
    when POOL_MEMORY.load()
        is @MemoryManager => return true
        is None => return false

### The manager the chunks come from: the one `set_up` named.
### :returns: The manager.
### :throws: Throws `UnacceptedValueError` when `set_up` wasn't called.
secret routine pool_memory() -> @MemoryManager
    when POOL_MEMORY.load()
        is @MemoryManager as memory => return memory
        is None =>
            throw UnacceptedValueError(description: "A stamp needs the PoolManager, and nothing set it up: call PoolManager.set_up(memory: HEAP) in start, before the first stamp().")

# A target with an operating system, or AArch64 without one (a ThreadManagerByRoundRobin), runs threads: the table's
# words are read and written unbroken and taking or ending a slot takes a short lock. Any other target without an
# operating system runs one flow and may have no unbroken access at all (a Cortex-M0 has none), so there the words are
# plain reads and writes and there is no lock.

### Takes the table's lock.
@target(os: not "none")
secret routine lock_pool() -> None
    loop
        if POOL_LOCK.swap_unbroken(1) == 0
            return

### Gives the table's lock back.
@target(os: not "none")
secret routine unlock_pool() -> None
    POOL_LOCK.poke_unbroken(0)
    return

### Reads a word of the table that another thread may write, unbroken.
### :typeparam T: The word's type.
### :param at: The word.
### :returns: Its value.
@target(os: not "none")
@inline
secret routine pool_read[T](at: @T) -> T
needs T obeys UnbrokenStorable
    return at.peek_unbroken[ACQUIRE]()

### Writes a word of the table that another thread may read, unbroken.
### :typeparam T: The word's type.
### :param at: The word.
### :param value: What it holds from now on.
@target(os: not "none")
@inline
secret routine pool_write[T](at: @T, value: T) -> None
needs T obeys UnbrokenStorable
    at.poke_unbroken(value)
    return

### Takes the table's lock.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine lock_pool() -> None
    loop
        if POOL_LOCK.swap_unbroken(1) == 0
            return

### Gives the table's lock back.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine unlock_pool() -> None
    POOL_LOCK.poke_unbroken(0)
    return

### Reads a word of the table that another thread may write, unbroken.
### :typeparam T: The word's type.
### :param at: The word.
### :returns: Its value.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
@inline
secret routine pool_read[T](at: @T) -> T
needs T obeys UnbrokenStorable
    return at.peek_unbroken[ACQUIRE]()

### Writes a word of the table that another thread may read, unbroken.
### :typeparam T: The word's type.
### :param at: The word.
### :param value: What it holds from now on.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
@inline
secret routine pool_write[T](at: @T, value: T) -> None
needs T obeys UnbrokenStorable
    at.poke_unbroken(value)
    return

### Takes the table's lock: one flow, nothing to wait for.
@target(arch: not "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine lock_pool() -> None
    return

### Gives the table's lock back: one flow, nothing to give.
@target(arch: not "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine unlock_pool() -> None
    return

### Reads a word of the table: one flow, a plain read.
### :typeparam T: The word's type.
### :param at: The word.
### :returns: Its value.
@target(arch: not "aarch64", os: "none")
@inline
secret routine pool_read[T](at: @T) -> T
    return at.peek()

### Writes a word of the table: one flow, a plain write.
### :typeparam T: The word's type.
### :param at: The word.
### :param value: What it holds from now on.
@target(arch: not "aarch64", os: "none")
@inline
secret routine pool_write[T](at: @T, value: T) -> None
    at.poke(value)
    return

### The entry of a slot that was taken once: the chunk from the directory, then the entry in it. No lock.
### :param slot: The slot, not 0.
### :returns: The entry.
@inline
secret routine pool_entry(slot: U32) -> @PoolEntry
    directory: @Addr = pool_read(POOL_DIRECTORY).to[@Addr]()
    chunk: Addr = pool_read(directory.stride((slot >> 12).to[U64]()))
    return chunk.to[@PoolEntry]().stride((slot & 4095).to[U64]())

### Adds a chunk, and a directory twice the size when the directory is full. The directory it replaces stays. Called
### with the lock held.
### :throws: Throws `OutOfMemoryError` when the manager has no memory.
secret routine add_pool_chunk() -> None
    memory: @MemoryManager = pool_memory()
    chunks: U64 = POOL_CHUNKS.load()
    room: U64 = POOL_DIRECTORY_ROOM.load()
    if chunks == room
        grown: U64 = when
            room == 0 => 4
            else => room * 2
        fresh: Addr = allocate[Addr](count: grown, manager: memory)
        if fresh.is_hollow()
            throw OutOfMemoryError()
        if chunks > 0
            fresh.to[@Addr]().copy(src: POOL_DIRECTORY.load().to[@Addr](), count: chunks)
        pool_write(POOL_DIRECTORY, value: fresh)
        POOL_DIRECTORY_ROOM.store(grown)
    chunk: Addr = allocate[PoolEntry](count: POOL_CHUNK_SLOTS, manager: memory)
    if chunk.is_hollow()
        throw OutOfMemoryError()
    chunk.to[@PoolEntry]().zeroinit(POOL_CHUNK_SLOTS)
    directory: @Addr = POOL_DIRECTORY.load().to[@Addr]()
    pool_write(directory.stride(chunks), value: chunk)
    POOL_CHUNKS.store(chunks + 1)
    return

### Takes a slot for the entity at `at`: a free one, else the next one never used. For a language runtime that keeps
### the slot itself (Revare's): a Lapila program takes stamps with `stamp()`.
### :param at: The entity's address.
### :returns: The slot.
### :throws: Throws `OutOfMemoryError` when there is no memory for a chunk, or every slot is taken.
common routine PoolManager.take(at: Addr) -> U32
    lock_pool()
    free: U32 = POOL_FREE.load()
    if free != 0
        entry: @PoolEntry = pool_entry(free)
        POOL_FREE.store(entry.next_free.peek())
        entry.next_free.poke(0)
        pool_write(entry.at, value: at)
        unlock_pool()
        return free
    next: U32 = when
        POOL_NEXT.load() == 0 => 1
        else => POOL_NEXT.load()
    if next == U32.MAX
        unlock_pool()
        throw OutOfMemoryError()
    if (next >> 12).to[U64]() == POOL_CHUNKS.load()
        add_pool_chunk()
    POOL_NEXT.store(next + 1)
    pool_write(pool_entry(next).at, value: at)
    unlock_pool()
    return next

### The generation of a slot that is taken. For a language runtime that keeps the slot itself.
### :param slot: The slot.
### :returns: Its generation.
@inline
common routine PoolManager.generation(slot: U32) -> U32
    return pool_read(pool_entry(slot).generation)

### Ends the entity in a slot: its address is cleared and its generation goes up, so its stamps fail, and the slot is
### free for the next stamp. A slot whose generation reaches U32.MAX is retired instead. For a language runtime that
### keeps the slot itself.
### :param slot: The slot.
common routine PoolManager.end(slot: U32) -> None
    lock_pool()
    entry: @PoolEntry = pool_entry(slot)
    pool_write(entry.at, value: Addr.zero())
    generation: U32 = entry.generation.peek()
    if generation == U32.MAX
        unlock_pool()
        return
    pool_write(entry.generation, value: generation + 1)
    entry.next_free.poke(POOL_FREE.load())
    POOL_FREE.store(slot)
    unlock_pool()
    return

### The address of the entity a stamp was taken of, or zero when it has ended or is between places. One read of the
### entry: on one thread nothing ends the entity between the reads. For a language runtime that keeps the slot
### itself: a Lapila program reads a stamp with `fetch()`.
### :param slot: The stamp's slot.
### :param generation: The stamp's generation.
### :returns: The address, or zero.
@inline
common routine PoolManager.lookup(slot: U32, generation: U32) -> Addr
    # A slot past the last one taken (a stamp made by hand) has no entry, and slot 0's entry is all zero. Below
    # POOL_NEXT the directory and the slot's chunk exist.
    if slot >= POOL_NEXT.load()
        return Addr.zero()
    directory: @Addr = bitcast[Addr, @Addr](POOL_DIRECTORY.load())
    chunk: Addr = directory.stride((slot >> 12).to[U64]()).peek()
    entry: @PoolEntry = bitcast[Addr, @PoolEntry](chunk).stride((slot & 4095).to[U64]())
    if entry.generation.peek() != generation
        return Addr.zero()
    return entry.at.peek()

### A stamp of the entity `me` points at: its slot in the table, taken on the first stamp and kept by the entity, and
### the slot's generation. The slot gets the entity's address again, the one `me` names. A `&T` lends its view, so
### `node.stamp()` works on the place itself.
### :typeparam T: The entity's type.
### :returns: The stamp.
### :throws: Crashes with `OutOfMemoryError` when the table has no memory for a new slot.
@inline
routine PointedCell[T].stamp() -> Stamped[T]
    slot: @U32 = stamp_slot[T](me)
    taken: U32 = slot.peek()
    if taken != 0
        entry: @PoolEntry = pool_entry(taken)
        pool_write(entry.at, value: me)
        return Stamped[T](slot: taken, generation: entry.generation.peek())
    fresh: U32 = PoolManager.take(at: me)
    slot.poke(fresh)
    return Stamped[T](slot: fresh, generation: PoolManager.generation(fresh))

### A stamp of the entity in the place: `getview().stamp()`.
### :typeparam T: The entity's type.
### :returns: The stamp.
### :throws: Crashes with `OutOfMemoryError` when the table has no memory for a new slot.
routine @BorrowedCell[T].stamp() -> Stamped[T]
    return me.getview().stamp()

### The entity the stamp was taken of, wherever it lives now.
### :typeparam T: The entity's type.
### :returns: The entity's view.
### :throws: `StaleStampError` when the entity has ended, or is between places (taken out of one and not yet in another).
routine Stamped[T].fetch() -> @T
    at: Addr = PoolManager.lookup(slot: me.slot, generation: me.generation)
    if at.is_hollow()
        throw StaleStampError()
    return at.to[@T]()

### Whether two stamps are of the same entity, the same slot and generation: `a === b`. A stamp names an entity, so it
### compares by identity and has no `==`.
### :typeparam T: The entity's type.
### :param other: The other stamp.
### :returns: True for the same slot and generation.
routine Stamped[T].same(other: Me) -> Bool
    return me.slot == other.slot and me.generation == other.generation

### Whether two stamps are of different entities: `a !== b`.
### :typeparam T: The entity's type.
### :param other: The other stamp.
### :returns: True when the slot or the generation differs.
routine Stamped[T].notsame(other: Me) -> Bool
    return me.slot != other.slot or me.generation != other.generation

### Ends the slot of the entity `at` points at, if it has one, and clears the entity's hidden slot: the builder calls it
### first thing in the entity's destruct.
### :typeparam T: The entity's type.
### :param at: The entity.
secret routine release_stamp[T](at: @T) -> None
    when stamp_slot_kept[T](at)
        is @U32 as slot =>
            taken: U32 = slot.peek()
            if taken == 0
                return
            slot.poke(0)
            PoolManager.end(taken)
            return
        is None => return

### Clears the address of a slot whose entity left its place: the builder calls it where a value that holds one is
### taken out (steal, the return of a STACK place). Its stamps fail until the entity lands in another place.
### :param slot: The slot the entity keeps, 0 for none.
secret routine detach_stamp_slot(slot: U32) -> None
    if slot == 0
        return
    pool_write(pool_entry(slot).at, value: Addr.zero())
    return

### Gives the slot of the entity `at` points at its new address, if it has one: the builder calls it where a value that
### holds the entity is written to memory.
### :typeparam T: The entity's type.
### :param at: The entity, at its new address.
secret routine move_stamp[T](at: @T) -> None
    when stamp_slot_kept[T](at)
        is @U32 as slot =>
            taken: U32 = slot.peek()
            if taken == 0
                return
            pool_write(pool_entry(taken).at, value: at)
            return
        is None => return

### Gives the stamped entities of `count` values in a row their new address: what a collection calls once it moved
### them by bytes (a reallocation, a copy of raw memory), so their stamps keep working. Nothing, built to nothing, for a
### type that holds no stamped entity.
### :typeparam T: The values' type.
### :param at: The first value, at its new address.
### :param count: How many there are.
routine move_stamps[T](at: @T, count: U64) -> None
    if not holds_stamps[T]()
        return
    for i in 0 til count
        follow_stamps[T](at.stride(i))
    return

### Ends the stamps of `count` entities in a row, for a collection that ends them without their destruct. Nothing for
### a type the program doesn't stamp.
### :typeparam T: The entities' type.
### :param at: The first entity.
### :param count: How many there are.
routine release_stamps[T](at: @T, count: U64) -> None
    when stamp_slot_kept[T](at)
        is @U32 => nothing
        is None => return
    for i in 0 til count
        release_stamp[T](at.stride(i))
    return
